@prefix ds: <https://ds.canonical.com/>.
@prefix anatomy: <https://anatomy.canonical.com/>.
@prefix dt: <https://dt.canonical.com/>.

ds:apps_landscape.component.cron_schedule a ds:Component;
    ds:name "Cron Schedule";
    ds:summary "Cron Schedule is an Input-based form component for authoring five-field cron expressions. It translates a valid value into inline English help and includes a help trigger in its default label that opens an in-product syntax reference.\n\nMain use cases:\n\n- Workflows that intentionally expose five-field cron syntax.  \n- Users need immediate feedback (phrase translation) while editing cron intervals.  \n- In-product reference when users cannot access external crontab documentation.\n\n**Limitations:** Not a complete validator. Reads only first five ASCII-space-separated fields, ignores additional fields, non-whitespace delimiters, and zero/negative steps. Always validate on the server side.\n\n";
    ds:anatomyDsl "node:\n  uri: ds:apps_landscape.component.cron_schedule\n  role: cron expression input\n  edges:\n    - node:\n        role: default label cluster\n        relation:\n          cardinality: \"0..1\"\n          condition: label prop does not replace the default\n        edges:\n          - node:\n              role: label text\n              content: Schedule\n              relation:\n                cardinality: \"1\"\n          - node:\n              uri: global.component.button\n              role: cron help trigger\n              a11y:\n                aria-label: Open cron help\n              edges:\n                - node:\n                    uri: global.component.icon\n                    name: help\n                    relation:\n                      cardinality: \"1\"\n                - node:\n                    uri: global.component.modal\n                    role: cron reference dialog\n                    relation:\n                      cardinality: \"0..1\"\n                      condition: isModalOpen\n                    edges:\n                      - node:\n                          role: example section\n                          edges:\n                            - node:\n                                role: cron example part\n                                relation:\n                                  cardinality: \"5\"\n                            - node:\n                                role: example prose\n                                relation:\n                                  cardinality: \"1\"\n                          relation:\n                            cardinality: \"1\"\n                      - node:\n                          role: accepted values section\n                          edges:\n                            - node:\n                                uri: global.component.main-table\n                                relation:\n                                  cardinality: \"2\"\n                          relation:\n                            cardinality: \"1\"\n              relation:\n                cardinality: \"1\"\n    - node:\n        role: help phrase\n        relation:\n          cardinality: \"0..1\"\n          condition: toCronPhrase returns successfully\n    - node:\n        role: error message\n        relation:\n          cardinality: \"0..1\"\n          condition: toCronPhrase throws Error and touched is truthy";
    ds:anatomyClassic "https://www.figma.com/design/I4WpvwMuQI6v1qhgUl63Sx/Landscape-Pragma-components?node-id=17-20&t=iG2ObZ6Q1XG2A2MB-4\n\nTable 17";
    ds:usage "### When to use\n\n**Five-field cron authoring**\n\nUse when a workflow stores the same minute, hour, day-of-month, month, and day-of-week grammar represented by the helper and reference modal.\n\n**Teach-while-typing**  \nUse the computed phrase as immediate interpretation of a syntactically accepted value. Do not treat it as proof that the execution engine will run with identical semantics.\n\n**In-context reference**  \nUse the built-in help trigger when you cannot rely on users leaving the app to read crontab documentation.\n\n### When not to use\n\n**Constrained schedules**\n\nThe product supports only a fixed set of named schedule presets like “Hourly”, “Daily”, or “Weekly”.\n\n**Incorrect pattern**: Exposing full five-field cron syntax when the business logic allows only a few predefined options.\n\n**Remediation strategy**: Use radio buttons or a select dropdown with preset labels; map the selected preset to a cron value at the API boundary if needed, but do not expose raw cron to users.\n\n**Non-cron schedulers**\n\nThe backend uses calendar RRule, systemd OnCalendar grammar, business-hours pickers, or another scheduling format.\n\n**Incorrect pattern**: Using Cron Schedule for calendar RFC RRule fields or minute-less UIs when the backend does not execute cron semantics.\n\n**Remediation strategy**: Use or build a scheduler component tailored to the target format; do not translate between formats at the UI level unless the API explicitly requires it.\n\n**Execution validation**\n\nThe `toCronPhrase` output must be treated as validation that the job will execute as expected.\n\n**Incorrect pattern**: Relying solely on `toCronPhrase` returning a phrase as proof that the cron interval is correct and will run on the target system.\n\n**Remediation strategy**: Validate on the server side; use `toCronPhrase` only as UX interpretation. Additional fields are ignored, non-positive steps are not rejected, and execution engine behavior remains the server’s responsibility.\n\n**Locale-critical legal or compliance copy**\n\nSchedule prose must be provided in the user’s language and cannot be hardcoded in English.\n\n**Incorrect pattern**: Using `toCronPhrase` as-is in a compliance-sensitive workflow that requires translated schedule prose.\n\n**Remediation strategy**: Either extend the component to support i18n translations of month/day names, ordinals, and connectors, or use a different scheduler component until localization is implemented.";
    ds:guidelines "### Accessibility\n\n- The help trigger has an explicit accessible name and `type=\"button\"`; the icon has no local `aria-hidden` prop, so its final accessibility behavior depends on Canonical `Icon`.\n- Dialog semantics, focus trap, Escape behavior, and focus restoration are delegated to Canonical `Modal` and are not asserted by local tests.\n- Error/help association is delegated to Canonical `Input`; local tests verify visible text, not computed ARIA relationships or announcements.\n- Example parts in the modal are visual teaching aids; the prose sentence under the example must carry the meaning, not the large numbers alone.\n\n### Content\n\n- Help phrase should read like a confirmation sentence, not a debug dump.\n- Error messages are short and actionable (“Enter a complete interval.”, “Value out of range.”, “Invalid character.”).\n- Modal copy should stay reference-like: operators table + accepted ranges + one worked example.\n\n### Internationalization / globalization\n\n- Default label, modal title, section headings, table content, example prose, and trigger accessible name are hardcoded English with no props for replacement.\n- `toCronPhrase` hardcodes English month/day names, ordinals, and connectors such as “at”, “past”, “on”, “and on”, “in”, and “every”.\n- Ordinal suffixes (`st`, `nd`, `rd`, `th`) are English-specific.\n\n### Animation\n\n- Source adds no local animation. Any modal animation belongs to Canonical `Modal`.\n- Parsing occurs synchronously on every render. Valid help and touched errors replace one another immediately.\n\n### Keyboard navigation\n\n- Field: standard text input tab stop and editing.\n- The native button should activate with Enter/Space. Local tests cover pointer clicks only.\n- Modal keyboard behavior is dependency-owned and is not proven by this dossier’s tests.\n\n### Device / layout\n\n- Cron Help sets the example row to `display: flex` with a `2rem` gap and no wrapping rule.\n- Both tables have a fixed `38rem` width. There is no component breakpoint, max-width, horizontal-scroll wrapper, or narrow-screen adaptation in local styles.\n- The input’s responsive behavior comes from Canonical `Input` and its containing layout.\n\n### Security / correctness\n\n- Treat phrase generation as UX interpretation, not a substitute for server-side and execution-engine validation.\n- The helper joins non-wildcard day-of-month and day-of-week phrases with “and on”; it does not establish whether a target cron engine executes those fields with AND or OR semantics.\n- A non-`Error` thrown during parsing causes `CronSchedule` to return `undefined` and render nothing. Helper code currently throws `Error`, but the fallback is not tested.";
    ds:tier ds:apps_landscape;
    ds:documentationStage ds:tag.specialadoption-docs;
    ds:hasProperty [
        a ds:Property;
        ds:name "**CronSchedule**";
        ds:optional "false"
    ], [
        a ds:Property;
        ds:name "value";
        ds:summary "Inherited native Input value passed to the field and converted with `.toString()` for parsing.";
        ds:optional "false";
        ds:propertyType "text | number | list of text";
        ds:constraints "Intended usage is a cron string with five ASCII-space-separated fields. Numbers and arrays remain type-valid inherited values but do not provide the intended cron contract; arrays stringify with commas. "
    ], [
        a ds:Property;
        ds:name "touched";
        ds:summary " When true, parse failures are shown as Input error. When false, failures stay silent while help may be empty. ";
        ds:optional "true";
        ds:propertyType "boolean";
        ds:defaultValue "undefined / falsy"
    ], [
        a ds:Property;
        ds:name "inputProps";
        ds:summary " Forwards Input props including handlers, name, disabled, required, label, and type. ";
        ds:optional "true";
        ds:propertyType "object";
        ds:constraints "`label` and `type` override local defaults because they are spread later. `help`, `value`, and `error` are then overwritten by computed props. This is Canonical Input's `ComponentProps<typeof Input>`.";
        ds:defaultValue "Canonical Input defaults, except local label/type defaults"
    ].
